###  https://purl.archive.org/nudging#urban_wage_premium
<https://purl.archive.org/nudging#urban_wage_premium> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                               <http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dul/DUL.owl#Theory> ;
                                                      rdfs:comment "In high-income countries, researchers have consistently documented a strong positive relationship between a city-region’s density and size, and its average income level. A nominal wage increase workers experience when located in more urbanized areas, and which is generally attributed to higher productivity in such locations. Research suggests this is not simply due to the selection of certain types of workers into more urbanized areas, but that workers match to jobs better and gain more valuable skills and experience in more urbanized areas. Research has focused especially on disparities across education levels. There is an increasing gap in the urban wage premium for college-educated and non-college-educated workers, leading some to question whether an urban wage premium continues to exist for non-college-educated workers."@en ;
                                                      rdfs:label "urban wage premium" ;
                                                      <https://purl.archive.org/nudging#Demonstrates> <https://purl.archive.org/nudging#urbanization_intensifies_inequality> .


